   <text>
      <body>
         <div type="letter">
                <div type="address">
                    <ab>
                        <address>
                            <addrLine>
                                <name type="geo" key="G_CHE_ZURI_06">Seefeldstrasse 73</name> <hi rend="superscript">III</hi>
                            </addrLine>
                            <addrLine>
                                <name type="geo" key="G_CHE_ZURI">Zürich</name>
                            </addrLine> </address>
                    </ab>
                </div> <div type="mainBody">
                    <opener>
                        <salute>Dear <name type="person" key="weav76">Miss Weaver</name>
                        </salute>
                    </opener>
                    <p>        	I received last Saturday your telegraphic remittance of £10 and this morning your cheque for £15 for both of which <rs type="person" key="joyc82">my husband</rs> sends you his very grateful thanks. Kindly excuse me for not having written ere now but I waited a letter in confirmation of your wire.</p>
                    <p>        	<rs type="person" key="joyc82">My husband</rs> is now at home again but will be convalescent for two or three weeks still. The operation was satisfactory and we hope he will now not be troubled with a recurrence of <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">these attacks</seg>. He was very glad indeed to hear <seg xml:id="seg2" type="note-anchor">the good news</seg> in your letter and thanks you for your kind and generous patronage of his <name type="work" key="W_ULYSSES">
                            <hi rend="underline">Ulysses</hi>
                        </name>.</p>
                    <closer>
                        <salute>                    	yours sincerely</salute>
                        <signed>
                            <name type="person" key="barn84">                                	Nora Joyce</name>
                        </signed> <dateline>
                            <date when="1917-09-15">15.9.1917</date>
                        </dateline> </closer>
                </div> </div>
            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary">JJ had undergone an eye operation after suffering a severe attack of glaucoma on the evening of <date when="1917-08-18">August 18</date> (<name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">JJ</name> 417).</note>
                <note xml:id="note2" target="#seg2" type="commentary">Thinking that <name type="work" key="W_ULYSSES">Ulysses</name> might be finished in <date when="1918-01">January 1918</date>, JJ had suggested to Ezra Pound that it could appear as a serial in <name type="work" key="W_THEEGOIST">The Egoist</name> and <name type="work">The Little Review</name> simultaneously. In her letter of <date when="1917-09-06">6 September 1917</date>, Harriet Weaver wrote to JJ that she would be glad to have <name type="work" key="W_ULYSSES">Ulysses</name> for <name type="work" key="W_THEEGOIST">The Egoist</name> and that the journal could pay him £50. At Pound’s suggestion, Weaver telegraphed ten pounds as a first instalment two days previously and sent JJ a cheque for £15 with her letter of 6 September. In the same letter, Weaver notified JJ that she might have found “a country printer willing to print the second edition” of <name type="work" key="W_APORTRAIT">A Portrait of the Artist as a Young Man</name>, “a Southport firm which printed the <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">Egoist</name> till the publication was transferred to London in July 1914”, i.e., Robert Johnson and Co., Ltd. (Firth 177).</note>
            </div>
      </body>
